ancies of paths or spanning trees in structured graphs like grids, this research investigates the set $\\mathcal{T}$ of all subtrees within a base tree $T$. ... The most significant finding is that the discrepancy of subtrees depends almost entirely on the number of leaves $\\ell$ in the base tree, rather than the total number of vertices. ... Additionally, they propose a conjecture for \"complex discrepancy\" (colors on the unit circle), suggesting a bound of $\\ell/\\pi$.\n\n# Discrepancies of subtrees\n\nTarun Krishna, Peleg Michaeli, Michail Sarantis, Fenglin Wang, Yiqing WangarXiv 2023 · DOI · arXiv\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe study multicolour, oriented and high-dimensional discrepancies of the set of all subtrees of a tree. As our main result, we show that the $r$-colour discrepancy of the subtrees of any tree is a linear function of the number of leaves $\\ell$ of that tree. More concretely, we show that it is bounded by $\\lceil(r-1)\\ell/r\\rceil$ from below and $\\lceil(r-1)\\ell/2\\rceil$ from above, and that these bounds are asymptotically sharp. Motivated by this result, we introduce natural notions of oriented and high-dimensional discrepancies and prove bounds for the corresponding discrepancies of the set of all subtrees of a given tree as functions of its number of leaves.\n\n## Introduction\n\nCombinatorial discrepancy theory explores the limits of balance within set systems. Given a hypergraph, the goal is to color its elements (typically vertices or edges) such that every hyperedge contains a nearly equal distribution of colors. \n## Results\n\nThe research establishes several tight bounds:\n\n  * Multi-color Discrepancy: For a tree with $\\ell$ leaves and $r$ colors, the discrepancy $\\mathcal{D}_r(T, \\mathcal{T})$ satisfies: $$\\lceil (r-1)\\ell/r \\rceil \\le \\mathcal{D}_r(T, \\mathcal{T}) \\le \\lceil (r-1)\\ell/2 \\rceil$$ For $r=2$, the discrepancy is exactly $\\lceil \\ell/2 \\rceil$.\n\n  * Oriented Discrepancy: For directed rooted trees, the discrepancy is bounded between $\\lceil \\ell/2 \\rceil + 1$ and $\\ell$.\n\n  * High-Dimensional Bound: The $d$-dimensional discrepancy is bounded below by a function of the leaves and the Beta function: $\\mathcal{D}^d(T, \\mathcal{T}) \\ge \\frac{\\ell}{d \\cdot B(d/2, 1/2)}$.\n\nThese results also lead to improved lower bounds for discrepancy in $m \\times n$ grids, specifically $\\frac{r-1}{4r}mn + 1 - 2r$.\n\n## Conclusion and Future Directions\n\nWhile this work provides sharp bounds for multi-color discrepancy, gaps remain in the oriented and high-dimensional cases. The authors conjecture that the oriented discrepancy is closer to $\\ell/2$ than $\\ell$. Additionally, they propose a conjecture for \"complex discrepancy\" (colors on the unit circle), suggesting a bound of $\\ell/\\pi$. Future research may also focus on finding matching upper bounds for the high-dimensional geometric discrepancy to determine its exact sharpness.\n\nBibTeX\n    \n    @misc{krishna2023discrepancies,\n      author = {Tarun Krishna and Peleg Michaeli and Michail Sarantis and Fenglin Wang and Yiqing Wang},\n      title = {{Discrepancies of subtrees}},\n      year = {2023},\n      eprint = {2302.08557},\n      archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n      url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.08557},\n    }--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNew bounds on the maximum number of neighborly boxes in Rd (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0195669823001142)\nciteturn0search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.9 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; Let us conclude the paper with some open problems and suggestions for future studies of the function n(k,d)$n(k,d)$. ... The construction is recursive in nature and uses a kind of “algebra” on lists of ternary strings, which encode neighborly boxes in a familiar way. Moreover, we conjecture that our construction is optimal and gives an explicit formula for n(2,d)$n(2,d)$.\n## Final comments\n\nLet us conclude the paper with some open problems and suggestions for future studies of the function n(k,d)$n(k,d)$.\n\nConjecture 1\n\nFor every 1⩽k⩽d$1⩽k⩽d$, there exists a k$k$-neighborly total lamination F$F$ in {S}^{d}${S}^{d}$ of size n(k,d)$n(k,d)$.\n\nAs mentioned above, the conjecture is trivially true for k=1$k=1$ and k=d$k=d$. By the proof of Proposition 1 it is also true for k=d-1$k=d-1$. The statement holds also in all cases for which the exact value of n(k,d)$n(k,d)$ is known, except for n(6,8)=150$n(6,8)=150$, where the only construction known to us is not a total lamination. In\n\n## References (12)\n\n  * Cioabă S.M. et al.\n\n### Variations on a theme of Graham and Pollak\n\n### Discrete Math.\n\n(2013)\n\n  * Kleitman D.J.\n\n### On a combinatorial conjecture of Erdős\n\n### J. Combin. Theory\n\n(1966)\n\n  * Alon N.\n\n### Neighborly families of boxes and bipartite coverings\n\n  * Alon N. et al.\n\n### The Probabilistic Method\n\n(2016)\n\n  * Bezrukov S.L.\n\n### Specification of all maximal subsets of the unit cube with respect to given diameter\n\nIn this paper we are dealing with the case k=2$k=2$. We give a new construction of k$k$-neighborly codes giving better lower bounds on n(2,d)$n(2,d)$. The construction is recursive in nature and uses a kind of “algebra” on lists of ternary strings, which encode neighborly boxes in a familiar way. Moreover, we conjecture that our construction is optimal and gives an explicit formula for n(2,d)$n(2,d)$. This supposition is supported by some numerical experiments and some partial results on related open problems which are recalled.\n\n  * ### Exact values and improved bounds on k-neighborly families of boxes\n\n2024, European Journal of Combinatorics\n\nCitation Excerpt :\n\n[3] The following claim (also independently observed in [2]) is also crucial in our proof. We provide a simple proof for the sake of completeness.\n\nShow abstract\n\nA finite family \\mathcal{F}$\\mathcal{F}$ of d$d$-dimensional convex pol